

	General comments
	----------------

The LVG program is a generic radiative transfer program which supports
various molecules.  The list of supported molecules will hopefully
grow, but I will only add things I'm interested in.  The formalism is
large velocity gradient with Sobolev approximation.  

The program is written in C++ and optionally uses a library called
blitz++ to deal with arrays.  This library works only with the egcs
compiler above a certain version number which I've forgotten.  I have
only run and tested it on Linux, but it may work on other platforms as
well.  Since Linux is widely distributed, I am inclined to distribute
the binary rather than let the people compile themselves, although
interested persons can get the source if needed.

NEW: If you don't have blitz++ (or don't have Linux), it should be
possible to compile it.  Just comment out the lines referring to blitz
in generic/Makefile and type

   make noblitz

which should produce the file lvg_noblitz, which is the binary.
However, I use g++, which is available for free on all platforms, and
the program has only been tested with that compiler (there were
problems on our SUNs and HPs though).  If you have other c++
compilers, you have to modify the Makefile.  If it works, fine.  If
not, you're on your own: I'm not going to modify the code.

Setup: quite easy, if the binary is used.  You have to put the binary
(lvg) somewhere in your path, then the data files (.dat and .col
Files) somewhere else.  You have to point to that directory with the
environmental variable LVG_Data, e.g. with

setenv LVG_Data /home/schilke/transfer/lvg/Data  (for csh and tcsh)

       or

export LVG_Data=/home/schilke/transfer/lvg/Data   (bash and friends)

and then you're ready to run.  You can run either single calculations
or loops, but looping is not supported for all variables (right now
only temperature, H2 density and column density, if I remember
correctly).  A gui interface (lvg_gui using expectk, which should also exist
on every Linux station) is available for single calculations.

There are two output files: a <molecule>.out and a <molecule>.plt,
where <molecule> is the molecule used.  The .out file is a copy of the
screen output, the .plt file is a table to plot things using
e.g. GreG.  To use it, one has to know which columns are which.  This
information is written in a commented form (for GreG plots) in the first
line of the file, you have to figure out the column numbers yourself.

The feature of using internal dust is not recommended right now, I
have to look at this again, it may be broken.  But background HII
region or dust source are OK - in the present version they only play a
role in the output (i.e. absorption strengths etc.), their radiation
fields are at present NOT used in the calculation of the level
populations.

Collisions with electrons are only supported for HCO+.  

NOTE: NH3 doesn't work right now!!!
